.TH STTY 1
.SH NAME
stty \- get and set terminal line settings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B stty
.RB [ \-a | \-e | \-g ]
.RB [ \-F
.IR device " |"
.B \-f
.IR device ]
.br
.B stty
.RB [ \-F
.IR device " |"
.B \-f
.IR device ]
.IR operand ...
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B stty
utility reports or changes the line settings of a terminal device.
.PP
With no
.IR operand s
and no information option,
.B stty
writes to standard output the subset of settings that differ from
the
.RB \(lq sane \(rq
reference state.
.PP
With one or more
.IR operand s,
.B stty
parses every operand in order into a single working
.BR termios (3)
value and applies that value once with
.BR tcsetattr (3).
.PP
By default
.B stty
operates on the terminal open on standard input.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
Write all current settings to standard output in human-readable
grouped form.
.TP
.B \-e
Write all current settings in grouped form; a synonym of
.BR \-a .
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-save
Write all current settings to standard output as a single
.B gfmt1:
token suitable for being passed back to
.B stty
as an operand.
.TP
.BR \-F " " \fIdevice\fR ", " \-\-file " " \fIdevice\fR
Operate on
.I device
instead of standard input.
.TP
.BR \-f " " \fIdevice\fR
Operate on
.I device
instead of standard input (BSD spelling).
.TP
.B \-\-help
Write a usage summary to standard output and exit 0.
.TP
.B \-\-
Treat all following arguments as operands.
.SH OPERANDS
An operand naming a boolean mode turns that mode on; the same name
prefixed with
.B \-
turns it off.
.SS Control modes
.BR parenb ", " parodd ", " cs5 ", " cs6 ", " cs7 ", " cs8 ", "
.BR cstopb ", " cread ", " clocal ", " hupcl " (" hup ).
.SS Input modes
.BR ignbrk ", " brkint ", " ignpar ", " parmrk ", " inpck ", "
.BR istrip ", " inlcr ", " igncr ", " icrnl ", " ixon ", " ixany ", "
.BR ixoff " (" tandem ")," " imaxbel" ", " iuclc ", " iutf8 .
.SS Output modes
.BR opost ", " olcuc ", " onlcr ", " ocrnl ", " onocr ", "
.BR onlret ", " ofill ", " ofdel .
.SS Local modes
.BR isig ", " icanon ", " iexten ", " echo ", " echoe " (" crterase ")," " echok ,"
.BR echonl ", " noflsh ", " tostop ", " echoctl " (" ctlecho ")," " echoprt"
.RB "(" prterase ")," " echoke" " (" crtkill ")," " flusho" ", " pendin .
.SS Control characters
.BR intr ", " quit ", " erase ", " kill ", " eof ", " eol ", " eol2 ", "
.BR swtch ", " start ", " stop ", " susp ", " reprint " (" rprnt ")," " werase ,"
.BR lnext ", " discard " (" flush ).
Each takes a following value:
.B ^X
for a control character,
.B ^?
for delete,
.BR undef ", " ^\- ,
or an empty string to disable, or a numeric value
.RB ( 0x
hex,
.B 0
octal, otherwise decimal).
.SS Numeric and special operands
.TP
.I number
Set both input and output line speed to
.IR number .
.TP
.BR ispeed " " \fIn\fR ", " ospeed " " \fIn\fR
Set the input or output line speed.
.TP
.B speed
As the sole operand, print the output line speed and exit.
.TP
.B size
As the sole operand, print the terminal row and column count.
.TP
.BR rows " " \fIn\fR ", " cols " " \fIn\fR ", " columns " " \fIn\fR
Set the terminal window size.
.TP
.BR min " " \fIn\fR ", " time " " \fIn\fR
Set the non-canonical read parameters.
.TP
.BR line " " \fIn\fR
Set the line discipline number.
.TP
.BR drain ", " \-drain
Apply changes after output drains
.RB ( TCSADRAIN )
or immediately
.RB ( TCSANOW ).
.SS Combination operands
.TP
.B sane
Reset modes and control characters to the sane reference state.
.TP
.BR raw ", " \-raw ", " cooked ", " \-cooked
Place the terminal in raw or cooked mode.
.TP
.BR cbreak ", " \-cbreak
Disable or enable canonical input.
.TP
.BR nl ", " \-nl
Disable or enable carriage-return / newline translation.
.TP
.B ek
Reset
.B erase
and
.B kill
to their sane values.
.TP
.BR evenp ", " parity ", " oddp ", " \-parity
Enable even or odd parity, or disable parity.
.TP
.BR crt ", " dec
Enable display-friendly echo modes.
.TP
.BR pass8 ", " litout ", " decctlq
GNU 8-bit and flow-control combination shorthands.
.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
.B 0
All requested reporting and setting operations succeeded.
.TP
.B >0
An operand was invalid, a device could not be opened, the target
was not a terminal, or a setting could not be applied.
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
All diagnostics are written to standard error prefixed with the
program name
.BR stty .
A malformed
.B gfmt1:
save token is rejected without modifying the terminal.
.SH EXAMPLES
Disable input echo, then restore it:
.PP
.B stty \-echo
.br
.B stty echo
.PP
Save the terminal state, change it, and restore it exactly:
.PP
.B saved=$(stty \-g)
.br
.B stty raw \-echo
.br
.B stty "$saved"
.PP
Print the terminal size:
.PP
.B stty size
.SH CONFORMING TO
POSIX.1-2024.  GNU coreutils and BSD extensions are also accepted;
where the two extension sets conflict the BSD behaviour is used.
Output-delay operands, hardware-flow-control flags, and the
.BR xcase ,
.BR extproc ,
.BR erase2 ,
.BR dsusp ", and " status
operands are not supported on Substrate; see
.B docs/specs/stty.md
for the full conformance accounting.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR reset (1),
.BR tty (1),
.BR tcgetattr (3),
.BR tcsetattr (3),
.BR termios (3),
.BR ioctl (2)
